Part 1.

18
00:01:44.702 --> 00:01:46.622
Introduction and Why It Matters.

19
00:01:47.872 --> 00:01:50.688
A story: the slow coupon that took down the cart.

20
00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:58.968
On the first day of the Big Savings Sale, promotion-service started evaluating a new buy 2 get 1 rule with a slow database query.

21
00:01:59.248 --> 00:02:02.875
Coupon evaluation went from 40 ms to 4 seconds.

22
00:02:03.155 --> 00:02:12.925
What happened next took 23 minutes to understand: pricing-service called promotions with the HTTP client's default settings: no response timeout.

23
00:02:13.205 --> 00:02:21.760
Its 200 Tomcat threads filled up with requests waiting on promotions. checkout-service called pricing, and its own threads filled up too.

24
00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:26.072
So did the threads serving GET or cart, which doesn't need promotions at all.

25
00:02:26.352 --> 00:02:30.171
It shared the same thread pool and the same connection pool to pricing.

26
00:02:31.521 --> 00:02:34.294
The app gave up after 3 seconds and retried.

27
00:02:34.574 --> 00:02:41.337
The gateway retried twice, checkout retried pricing three times, and pricing retried promotions three times.

28
00:02:41.617 --> 00:02:47.419
The already struggling promotion database received ~27 times the normal query load.

29
00:02:47.699 --> 00:02:50.665
Even after users gave up, the queries kept running.

30
00:02:50.945 --> 00:02:59.329
Every layer used its own fixed timeout, so promotion-service kept evaluating coupons for requests nobody was waiting for any more.

31
00:02:59.609 --> 00:03:04.217
A 4-second slowdown in a non-critical feature became a full checkout outage.

32
00:03:04.497 --> 00:03:06.886
The fixes are the subject of this chapter:

33
00:03:08.236 --> 00:03:16.535
Every call has a timeout, shorter than the caller's own deadline, with a fallback where the data is optional (show MRP without the coupon).

34
00:03:16.815 --> 00:03:22.724
Deadlines propagate: the remaining time travels with the request, and every hop gives up when it's spent.

35
00:03:23.004 --> 00:03:29.703
Retries happen at one layer only, with a budget, and only for idempotent operations (Ch 18).

36
00:03:29.983 --> 00:03:36.063
Separate pools per dependency (bulkheads), so a slow dependency can't starve unrelated endpoints.

37
00:03:37.413 --> 00:03:38.095
Quote:

38
00:03:38.375 --> 00:03:40.039
The thesis of this chapter.

39
00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:46.677
A remote call is a request for someone else's time and capacity, under a deadline you're responsible for.

40
00:03:46.957 --> 00:03:58.477
Choose sync or async deliberately, pick a protocol and schema that evolves safely, and make how long to wait, how often to retry, and what to do instead an explicit part of every call.

