Part 2.

101
00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:30.567
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

102
00:06:31.817 --> 00:06:35.039
Intuition: phoning a supplier versus leaving a note.

103
00:06:35.319 --> 00:06:37.580
A dark-store manager needs more atta.

104
00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.508
Calling the supplier (synchronous): you wait on the line for an answer.

105
00:06:41.788 --> 00:06:45.649
If the supplier doesn't pick up, you're stuck until you decide to hang up.

106
00:06:45.929 --> 00:06:51.476
That's your timeout, and it must be shorter than your own deadline (the delivery van leaves at 5).

107
00:06:51.756 --> 00:06:59.137
If you keep redialling every few seconds, and so does every other store, the supplier's phone line collapses (retry storm).

108
00:06:59.417 --> 00:07:03.535
Leaving a note in the supplier's inbox (asynchronous): you don't wait.

109
00:07:03.815 --> 00:07:08.700
The supplier handles it when possible, and you get a reply later or see the stock arrive.

110
00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:11.241
That's good when you don't need the answer right now.

111
00:07:12.591 --> 00:07:16.623
Telling the supplier I need this answered by 4:30 is a deadline.

112
00:07:16.903 --> 00:07:21.639
If the supplier sees it's already 4:40, they shouldn't bother preparing the quote.

113
00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:29.023
A switchboard operator who connects calls, retries on busy lines, and checks everyone's identity is a service mesh.

114
00:07:30.373 --> 00:07:31.483
Sync or async?

115
00:07:31.763 --> 00:07:33.128
