136
00:08:07.915 --> 00:08:08.896
If yes, then:

137
00:08:09.176 --> 00:08:12.184
Sync with a tight budget, or async + notify.

138
00:08:12.464 --> 00:08:13.467
If no, then:

139
00:08:13.747 --> 00:08:14.472
Either.

140
00:08:15.822 --> 00:08:20.579
Synchronous calls couple availability (Ch 16 L1) and capacity:

141
00:08:20.859 --> 00:08:24.358
Little's law: requests in flight = rate times latency.

142
00:08:24.638 --> 00:08:33.961
At 400 req per second, a dependency slowing from 50 ms to 5 s raises in-flight requests from 20 to 2,000 (L1).

143
00:08:34.241 --> 00:08:40.107
Every in-flight request holds resources: a thread (unless virtual), a connection, and memory.

144
00:08:40.387 --> 00:08:42.393
L1 measured it with real threads:

145
00:08:42.673 --> 00:08:53.937
4,000 calls that each wait 500 ms took 10,144 ms on a 200-thread pool and 551 ms with virtual threads.

146
00:08:55.287 --> 00:08:59.596
Virtual threads remove the thread ceiling, not the dependency's capacity.

147
00:08:59.876 --> 00:09:07.193
All 4,000 calls now hit pricing at once, so you still need a bound: a semaphore or bulkhead (Ch 18).

148
00:09:08.543 --> 00:09:10.079
REST over HTTP.

149
00:09:10.359 --> 00:09:24.098
REST or JSON over HTTP (Ch 04) is the default for external APIs and fine for most internal ones: universal tooling, human-readable, cacheable (GET), easy to debug.

