178
00:10:19.755 --> 00:10:21.782
HTTP details that matter:

179
00:10:22.062 --> 00:10:29.422
Connection pooling and keep-alive: new TCP or TLS handshakes per call cost 1 to 3 RTTs.

180
00:10:29.702 --> 00:10:32.987
Pool per dependency, and size the pool from Little's law.

181
00:10:33.267 --> 00:10:40.755
HTTP or 2: multiplexing avoids head-of-line blocking at the HTTP layer, and header compression helps.

182
00:10:41.035 --> 00:10:51.574
It still runs on one TCP connection, so TCP-level head-of-line blocking remains (HTTP or 3 or QUIC removes it, Ch 03).

183
00:10:51.854 --> 00:10:53.710
Status codes drive retries:

184
00:10:53.990 --> 00:11:04.401
429 or 503 are retryable with backoff (honour Retry-After), 4xx aren't, and 5xx only for idempotent requests.

185
00:11:04.681 --> 00:11:08.563
Idempotency keys for POSTs that may be retried (Ch 20).

186
00:11:09.913 --> 00:11:18.084
gRPC and protobuf. gRPC is an RPC framework over HTTP or 2 with protobuf schemas:

187
00:11:18.364 --> 00:11:23.633
A contract-first.proto file generates typed clients and servers in many languages.

188
00:11:23.913 --> 00:11:25.556
Compact binary encoding.

189
00:11:25.836 --> 00:11:38.017
L3: the same 12-item order is 706 bytes as JSON and 271 as protobuf (278 versus 199 after gzip).

190
00:11:38.297 --> 00:11:40.580
Parsing is also cheaper (no text).

191
00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:46.577
Streaming: server, client and bidirectional (rider location streams, Ch 47).

192
00:11:47.927 --> 00:11:57.421
Deadlines are built in: the client sets a deadline, it travels as the grpc-timeout header, and servers can check Context.current().getDeadline().

193
00:11:57.701 --> 00:11:59.941
That's L2 B's fix, standardized.

194
00:12:00.221 --> 00:12:01.373
