227
00:13:30.793 --> 00:13:43.167
The grocery platform uses REST at the edge (app ↔ gateway or BFF) and gRPC for internal high-frequency calls (checkout ↔ pricing or inventory).

228
00:13:43.447 --> 00:13:46.305
Events stay on Kafka with Avro (Ch 14).

229
00:13:47.655 --> 00:13:49.426
Schema evolution on the wire.

230
00:13:49.706 --> 00:13:58.218
Callers and callees deploy independently (Ch 16), so every change must work with both older and newer peers during rollout.

231
00:13:58.498 --> 00:14:01.506
L3 used real protoc encodes and decodes:

232
00:14:01.786 --> 00:14:09.487
Renaming a field is safe in protobuf: only numbers are on the wire. orderid, then id was invisible to old readers.

233
00:14:09.767 --> 00:14:15.634
Adding a field is safe: an old consumer decoding v2 bytes kept the unknown field 7:

234
00:14:15.914 --> 00:14:19.391
DIWALI50 instead of losing it (forward compatibility).

235
00:14:19.671 --> 00:14:25.069
A new consumer decoding v1 bytes saw couponcode absent (backward compatibility).

236
00:14:25.349 --> 00:14:27.823
Code must treat absent or default correctly.

237
00:14:29.173 --> 00:14:36.107
Changing a field's type is not safe: int64 totalpaise, then string totalpaise under the same number.

238
00:14:36.387 --> 00:14:41.976
The new schema decoded no total at all (the value survived only as unknown field 4:

239
00:14:42.256 --> 00:14:45.115
184750).

240
00:14:45.395 --> 00:14:49.128
Nothing failed, so consumers would silently see an empty total.

241
00:14:49.408 --> 00:14:53.248
Reserve old numbers (reserved 4;) and add a new field instead.

242
00:14:53.528 --> 00:15:03.875
For JSON or REST: additive changes and tolerant readers (ignore unknown fields, @JsonIgnoreProperties(ignoreUnknown = true)).

243
00:15:04.155 --> 00:15:11.109
Breaking changes go to a new version (/v2) with both served during migration (Ch 04).

244
00:15:12.459 --> 00:15:21.867
Enforce it in CI: buf breaking for protobuf, OpenAPI diff tools for REST, and consumer-driven contract tests (Ch 29).

245
00:15:23.217 --> 00:15:24.049
Timeouts.

246
00:15:24.329 --> 00:15:30.260
A call without a timeout waits as long as the callee is slow, and so do all the resources it holds.

247
00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:40.353
L2 A ran a checkout service with 20 worker threads serving or cart (local work) and or price (calls pricing), with pricing degraded to 5 s:

248
00:15:41.703 --> 00:15:43.069
Here is a quick table:

249
00:15:43.349 --> 00:15:49.749
Checkout's call to pricing, and /cart OK, and /cart p99, and /price OK.

250
00:15:50.029 --> 00:15:52.247
No timeout. /cart OK:

251
00:15:52.527 --> 00:15:55.855
20 or 500. /cart p99:

252
00:15:56.135 --> 00:16:00.103
2,002 ms (client gave up). /price OK:

253
00:16:00.383 --> 00:16:01.962
0 or 500.

254
00:16:02.242 --> 00:16:07.383
300 ms timeout + fallback (cached MRP). /cart OK:

255
00:16:07.663 --> 00:16:11.290
500 or 500. /cart p99:

256
00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:13.853
6 ms. /price OK:

257
00:16:14.133 --> 00:16:17.098
500 or 500 (degraded content).

258
00:16:18.448 --> 00:16:23.760
An endpoint that doesn't even use pricing failed, because the threads were all waiting on pricing.

259
00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:26.173
That's the §1.1 cascade.

260
00:16:26.453 --> 00:16:27.605
Kinds of timeouts:

261
00:16:27.885 --> 00:16:33.923
Connect (TCP or TLS setup): short, for example, 200 to 500 ms.

262
00:16:34.203 --> 00:16:37.659
Response or read (time to first byte or between bytes).

263
00:16:37.939 --> 00:16:39.027
Total per call.

264
00:16:39.307 --> 00:16:42.357
Overall request deadline (§2.7).

265
00:16:42.637 --> 00:16:47.331
Pool-acquire timeouts matter too: waiting for a free connection is also waiting.

266
00:16:48.681 --> 00:16:49.811
Setting values:

267
00:16:50.091 --> 00:16:54.209
Start from the caller's budget and the dependency's latency distribution.

268
00:16:54.489 --> 00:17:02.425
The timeout is roughly the dependency's p99.9 plus a margin, and never more than the caller's remaining budget.

269
00:17:02.705 --> 00:17:06.246
A timeout longer than the caller's deadline does nothing useful.

270
00:17:06.526 --> 00:17:12.499
A timeout shorter than the dependency's normal p99 turns normal latency into errors.

271
00:17:13.849 --> 00:17:27.460
Budgets split across parallel and sequential calls: in checkout's 1.2 s, the parallel pricing or inventory or cart calls get 300 ms, payment 600 ms, order 200 ms.

272
00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:30.282
Deadline propagation.

273
00:17:30.562 --> 00:17:33.207
Per-hop fixed timeouts don't know about each other.

274
00:17:33.487 --> 00:17:41.274
The app waits 500 ms, but pricing waits 1 s for promotions, and promotions keeps working for 1.5 s.

275
00:17:41.554 --> 00:17:45.501
When the user has given up, the rest of the chain is still working for nobody.

276
00:17:45.781 --> 00:17:54.186
L2 B, 300 requests with a 500 ms app budget, and 30 % of promotion calls taking 1.5 s:

277
00:17:55.536 --> 00:17:56.901
Here is a quick table:

278
00:17:57.181 --> 00:18:02.301
Strategy, and Answered, and Work finished after the user gave up, and Abandoned early.

279
00:18:02.581 --> 00:18:04.267
Fixed 1 s timeout per hop.

280
00:18:04.547 --> 00:18:05.379
Answered:

281
00:18:05.659 --> 00:18:06.939
214.

282
00:18:07.219 --> 00:18:09.309
Work finished after the user gave up:

283
00:18:09.589 --> 00:18:14.923
86 requests, 89,157 ms of wasted work.

284
00:18:15.203 --> 00:18:16.312
Abandoned early:

285
00:18:16.592 --> 00:18:17.339
0.

286
00:18:17.619 --> 00:18:20.456
Propagated deadline (X-Deadline-Epoch-Ms).

287
00:18:20.736 --> 00:18:21.568
Answered:

288
00:18:21.848 --> 00:18:23.149
215.

289
00:18:23.429 --> 00:18:25.520
Work finished after the user gave up:

290
00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:26.547
0.

291
00:18:26.827 --> 00:18:27.936
Abandoned early:

292
00:18:28.216 --> 00:18:29.219
86.

293
00:18:30.569 --> 00:18:32.659
The same users were served either way.

294
00:18:32.939 --> 00:18:35.051
Deadlines don't make slow things fast.

295
00:18:35.331 --> 00:18:40.942
They stop wasted work, which during an overload is the capacity that lets the system recover.

296
00:18:41.222 --> 00:18:42.225
How it works:

297
00:18:42.505 --> 00:18:45.726
The edge sets an absolute deadline (now + budget).

298
00:18:46.006 --> 00:18:51.510
Each hop reads it and computes remaining = deadline minus now minus margin.

299
00:18:51.790 --> 00:18:58.766
It refuses to start if remaining at most 0, sets downstream timeouts to remaining, and forwards the deadline.

300
00:19:00.116 --> 00:19:05.087
Long-running work checks the deadline periodically, or cancels on client disconnect.

301
00:19:05.367 --> 00:19:14.775
Details: gRPC does this natively (grpc-timeout carries the relative remaining time, which avoids clock-skew issues between machines).

302
00:19:15.055 --> 00:19:25.721
For HTTP, prefer a relative remaining ms header recomputed at each hop, or absolute epoch time only within a well-synchronized cluster (Ch 12).

303
00:19:26.001 --> 00:19:34.684
Databases can honour deadlines too: set the JDBC query timeout or SET LOCAL statementtimeout from the remaining budget.

304
00:19:36.034 --> 00:19:37.527
Retries without storms.

305
00:19:37.807 --> 00:19:47.685
Retries help with transient failures (a dropped connection, one bad instance) and hurt with overload, because they add load exactly when the callee is struggling.

306
00:19:47.965 --> 00:19:49.650
Layered retries multiply.

307
00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:55.861
L2 C, edge, then layer 1, then layer 2, then a failed leaf, 100 user requests:

308
00:19:57.211 --> 00:19:58.576
Here is a quick table:

309
00:19:58.856 --> 00:20:01.395
Retry placement, and Calls reaching the leaf.

310
00:20:01.675 --> 00:20:03.979
3 attempts at the edge and at each layer:

311
00:20:04.259 --> 00:20:06.925
2,700 (27x).

312
00:20:07.205 --> 00:20:08.848
3 attempts at the edge only:

313
00:20:09.128 --> 00:20:10.685
300 (3x).

314
00:20:12.035 --> 00:20:14.211
Rules (details in Ch 18):

315
00:20:14.491 --> 00:20:19.611
Retry at one layer, usually the one closest to the user or the mesh, not everywhere.

316
00:20:19.891 --> 00:20:24.158
Retry only idempotent operations, or use idempotency keys.

317
00:20:24.438 --> 00:20:31.307
Use exponential backoff with jitter, and a retry budget (for example, retries at most 10 % of requests).

318
00:20:31.587 --> 00:20:33.422
Never retry past the deadline.

319
00:20:34.772 --> 00:20:37.673
Honour Retry-After and 429s.

320
00:20:37.953 --> 00:20:41.644
Circuit breakers stop calling a dependency that's clearly down.

321
00:20:42.994 --> 00:20:44.018
Service mesh.

322
00:20:44.298 --> 00:20:50.698
A service mesh moves cross-cutting communication concerns out of application code into the network layer:

323
00:20:52.048 --> 00:20:53.413
Here is a quick table:

324
00:20:53.693 --> 00:20:58.813
Capability, and In the mesh, and Still in the app. mTLS and service identity.

325
00:20:59.093 --> 00:20:59.989
In the mesh:

326
00:21:00.269 --> 00:21:02.723
Automatic certificates, identity (SPIFFE).

327
00:21:03.003 --> 00:21:03.984
Still in the app:

328
00:21:04.264 --> 00:21:08.424
Authorization decisions using that identity (or mesh policies).

329
00:21:08.704 --> 00:21:10.069
Retries, timeouts.

330
00:21:10.349 --> 00:21:11.245
In the mesh:

331
00:21:11.525 --> 00:21:12.379
Per route.

332
00:21:12.659 --> 00:21:13.640
Still in the app:

333
00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:18.357
Deadlines and fallbacks need business knowledge; the app must know the call failed.

334
00:21:18.637 --> 00:21:20.920
Load balancing, outlier detection.

335
00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:22.096
In the mesh:

336
00:21:22.376 --> 00:21:25.341
Ejects bad instances (L2-style failures).

337
00:21:25.621 --> 00:21:27.797
Traffic shifting (canary, mirroring).

338
00:21:28.077 --> 00:21:28.973
In the mesh:

339
00:21:29.253 --> 00:21:32.283
Weighted routes, shadowing (Ch 16 strangler).

340
00:21:32.563 --> 00:21:33.544
Still in the app:

341
00:21:33.824 --> 00:21:35.104
Diffing responses.

342
00:21:35.384 --> 00:21:36.323
Telemetry.

343
00:21:36.603 --> 00:21:37.499
In the mesh:

344
00:21:37.779 --> 00:21:39.997
Golden metrics, trace spans per hop.

345
00:21:40.277 --> 00:21:41.259
Still in the app:

346
00:21:41.539 --> 00:21:43.267
Business context in traces.

347
00:21:44.617 --> 00:21:45.619
Rate limiting.

348
00:21:45.899 --> 00:21:46.795
In the mesh:

349
00:21:47.075 --> 00:21:47.907
Partial.

350
00:21:48.187 --> 00:21:49.169
Still in the app:

351
00:21:49.449 --> 00:21:50.558
Business quotas.

352
00:21:51.908 --> 00:21:53.551
Sidecar versus ambient:

353
00:21:54.901 --> 00:21:56.266
Here is a quick table:

354
00:21:56.546 --> 00:22:04.461
Sidecar (Istio classic, Linkerd), and Ambient or sidecarless (Istio ambient: ztunnel + waypoint; Cilium).

355
00:22:04.741 --> 00:22:06.021
Where the proxy runs.

356
00:22:06.301 --> 00:22:08.519
Sidecar (Istio classic, Linkerd):

357
00:22:08.799 --> 00:22:12.298
An Envoy or linkerd2-proxy container in every pod.

358
00:22:12.578 --> 00:22:17.549
Ambient or sidecarless (Istio ambient: ztunnel + waypoint; Cilium):

359
00:22:17.829 --> 00:22:24.250
L4 per-node proxy (ztunnel) for mTLS; optional per-namespace L7 waypoint.

360
00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:25.618
Resource cost.

361
00:22:25.898 --> 00:22:28.117
Sidecar (Istio classic, Linkerd):

362
00:22:28.397 --> 00:22:32.066
CPU or memory per pod, and it scales with pod count.

363
00:22:32.346 --> 00:22:37.317
Ambient or sidecarless (Istio ambient: ztunnel + waypoint; Cilium):

364
00:22:37.597 --> 00:22:38.386
Lower.

365
00:22:38.666 --> 00:22:41.525
L7 cost only where waypoints are enabled.

366
00:22:41.805 --> 00:22:42.722
Latency.

367
00:22:43.002 --> 00:22:45.221
Sidecar (Istio classic, Linkerd):

368
00:22:45.501 --> 00:22:48.295
Two extra proxy hops per call (out and in).

369
00:22:48.575 --> 00:22:53.546
Ambient or sidecarless (Istio ambient: ztunnel + waypoint; Cilium):

370
00:22:53.826 --> 00:22:56.983
L4 hop per node, and L7 only where needed.

371
00:22:57.263 --> 00:22:58.117
Upgrades.

372
00:22:58.397 --> 00:23:00.615
Sidecar (Istio classic, Linkerd):

373
00:23:00.895 --> 00:23:03.263
Restart every pod to update proxies.

374
00:23:03.543 --> 00:23:08.514
Ambient or sidecarless (Istio ambient: ztunnel + waypoint; Cilium):

375
00:23:08.794 --> 00:23:10.693
Upgrade node-level components.

376
00:23:10.973 --> 00:23:11.890
Maturity.

377
00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:14.389
Sidecar (Istio classic, Linkerd):

378
00:23:14.669 --> 00:23:15.714
Very mature.

379
00:23:15.994 --> 00:23:20.965
Ambient or sidecarless (Istio ambient: ztunnel + waypoint; Cilium):

380
00:23:21.245 --> 00:23:25.959
Istio ambient went GA in 2024, and features still vary.

381
00:23:26.239 --> 00:23:27.327
Check your version.

382
00:23:28.677 --> 00:23:29.787
What a mesh costs:

383
00:23:30.067 --> 00:23:31.944
Another control plane to operate.

384
00:23:32.224 --> 00:23:37.024
Latency (usually around a millisecond per hop, more with L7 processing).

385
00:23:37.304 --> 00:23:38.797
Debugging through proxies.

386
00:23:39.077 --> 00:23:45.456
Configuration that can conflict with application-level retries (double retries = L2 C).

387
00:23:46.806 --> 00:23:49.686
The rule: decide which layer owns each concern.

388
00:23:49.966 --> 00:23:55.171
Mesh: mTLS, outlier detection, basic timeouts and the retry policy.

389
00:23:55.451 --> 00:23:59.803
App: deadlines, fallbacks, idempotency and business-level retries.

390
00:24:00.083 --> 00:24:02.089
Never both retrying the same call.

391
00:24:03.439 --> 00:24:04.463
Other styles:

392
00:24:04.743 --> 00:24:07.644
GraphQL, WebSockets, async request-reply.

393
00:24:07.924 --> 00:24:14.239
GraphQL: clients ask for exactly the fields they need, and good for BFFs with varied screens.

394
00:24:14.519 --> 00:24:20.129
Watch N+1 resolution (use DataLoader batching), query cost limits, and caching.

395
00:24:20.409 --> 00:24:26.340
WebSocket or SSE: server push for live tracking and order status (Ch 08).

396
00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:35.431
Async request-reply: the caller sends a command with a correlation id and a reply topic, and receives the result as a message later.

397
00:24:35.711 --> 00:24:39.444
Useful for long operations (invoice PDF generation).

398
00:24:39.724 --> 00:24:41.345
HTTP equivalent:

399
00:24:41.625 --> 00:24:46.361
202 Accepted + a status URL + polling or webhook.

400
00:24:47.711 --> 00:24:56.373
Webhooks from external parties (Razorpay): verify signatures, respond fast (enqueue, then process), and dedup by event id.

