00:27:13.762 --> 00:27:14.402
1.

445
00:27:14.682 --> 00:27:16.090
Context and Problem.

446
00:27:16.370 --> 00:27:29.810
A 4 s slowdown in promotions cascaded into a checkout outage through missing timeouts, shared pools, layered retries (27x load) and work continuing after users gave up (§1.1).

447
00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:30.794
2.

448
00:27:31.074 --> 00:27:32.418
Goals or Non-Goals.

449
00:27:32.698 --> 00:27:46.415
Goals: a slow dependency degrades only the features that need it; no orphan work after deadlines; bounded retry amplification; schema changes never break a peer during rollout; uniform mTLS.

450
00:27:46.695 --> 00:27:54.055
Non-goals: replacing Kafka events with RPC; a mesh-managed L7 policy for every route on day one.

451
00:27:55.405 --> 00:27:56.152
3.

452
00:27:56.432 --> 00:27:57.392
Requirements.

453
00:27:58.742 --> 00:28:00.107
Here is a quick table:

454
00:28:00.387 --> 00:28:01.859
ID, and Requirement.

455
00:28:02.139 --> 00:28:03.185
NFR-1:

456
00:28:03.465 --> 00:28:11.017
Every outbound call has connect and response timeouts at most the remaining deadline (checked by a client library + lint).

457
00:28:11.297 --> 00:28:12.342
NFR-2:

458
00:28:12.622 --> 00:28:18.702
Deadlines propagate on all synchronous calls (grpc-timeout or X-Request-Timeout-Ms).

459
00:28:18.982 --> 00:28:20.049
NFR-3:

460
00:28:20.329 --> 00:28:26.601
Retries only at the gateway or mesh layer for idempotent calls, retry budget at most 10 %.

461
00:28:26.881 --> 00:28:27.969
NFR-4:

462
00:28:28.249 --> 00:28:32.963
Each dependency has its own connection pool and concurrency limit (bulkhead).

463
00:28:33.243 --> 00:28:34.289
NFR-5:

464
00:28:34.569 --> 00:28:41.609
Breaking API or proto changes are blocked in CI (buf breaking, OpenAPI diff, contract tests).

465
00:28:41.889 --> 00:28:42.977
NFR-6:

466
00:28:43.257 --> 00:28:47.929
All service-to-service traffic uses mTLS with workload identity.

