Timeouts from budgets, bulkheads per dependency, fallbacks.

734
00:46:36.909 --> 00:46:38.829
Orphan work after callers give up.

735
00:46:39.109 --> 00:46:40.304
Detection signal:

736
00:46:40.584 --> 00:46:44.275
Callee busy while callers time out; deadline-exceeded at the edge.

737
00:46:44.555 --> 00:46:45.771
Blast radius:

738
00:46:46.051 --> 00:46:49.059
Wasted capacity during overload (L2 B:

739
00:46:49.339 --> 00:46:50.512
89 s).

740
00:46:50.792 --> 00:46:51.795
Mitigation:

741
00:46:52.075 --> 00:46:55.744
Deadline propagation, cancel on deadline or disconnect.

742
00:46:56.024 --> 00:46:57.560
Retry amplification.

743
00:46:57.840 --> 00:46:59.035
Detection signal:

744
00:46:59.315 --> 00:47:02.195
Leaf QPS ≫ edge QPS during incidents.

745
00:47:02.475 --> 00:47:03.691
Blast radius:

746
00:47:03.971 --> 00:47:07.107
Overloaded dependency can't recover (L2 C:

747
00:47:07.387 --> 00:47:08.667
27x).

748
00:47:08.947 --> 00:47:09.949
Mitigation:

749
00:47:10.229 --> 00:47:13.003
One retry layer, budgets, breakers, jitter.

750
00:47:13.283 --> 00:47:15.352
Double retries (app + mesh).

751
00:47:15.632 --> 00:47:16.827
Detection signal:

752
00:47:17.107 --> 00:47:18.984
Retries per request 1 layer.

753
00:47:19.264 --> 00:47:20.480
Blast radius:

754
00:47:20.760 --> 00:47:21.805
Same as above.

755
00:47:22.085 --> 00:47:23.088
Mitigation:

756
00:47:23.368 --> 00:47:26.760
Decide an owner: mesh retries off or app retries off.

757
00:47:28.110 --> 00:47:29.582
Silent schema breakage.

758
00:47:29.862 --> 00:47:31.057
Detection signal:

759
00:47:31.337 --> 00:47:34.089
Default or empty values appearing, no errors.

760
00:47:34.369 --> 00:47:35.585
Blast radius:

761
00:47:35.865 --> 00:47:38.894
Wrong data propagated (L3: empty total).

762
00:47:39.174 --> 00:47:42.801
Mitigation: buf breaking, reserved numbers, contract tests.

763
00:47:43.081 --> 00:47:44.190
Connection churn.

764
00:47:44.470 --> 00:47:45.665
Detection signal:

765
00:47:45.945 --> 00:47:48.697
High TLS handshake rate, port exhaustion.

766
00:47:48.977 --> 00:47:50.193
Blast radius:

767
00:47:50.473 --> 00:47:52.030
Latency, CPU.

768
00:47:52.310 --> 00:47:53.313
Mitigation:

769
00:47:53.593 --> 00:47:56.153
Pooling, keep-alive, HTTP or 2.

770
00:47:56.433 --> 00:47:57.969
Mesh misconfiguration.

771
00:47:58.249 --> 00:47:59.443
Detection signal:

772
00:47:59.723 --> 00:48:04.438
Sudden 503s or mTLS errors after a policy change.

773
00:48:04.718 --> 00:48:05.934
Blast radius:

774
00:48:06.214 --> 00:48:07.366
Whole namespace.

775
00:48:07.646 --> 00:48:08.649
Mitigation:

776
00:48:08.929 --> 00:48:12.299
Staged rollout of mesh config, istioctl analyze.

777
00:48:12.579 --> 00:48:14.521
DNS caching of dead endpoints.

778
00:48:14.801 --> 00:48:15.995
Detection signal:

779
00:48:16.275 --> 00:48:18.707
Errors to one IP after a scale-down.

780
00:48:18.987 --> 00:48:20.203
Blast radius:

781
00:48:20.483 --> 00:48:21.785
1 or N of calls.

782
00:48:22.065 --> 00:48:23.067
Mitigation:

783
00:48:23.347 --> 00:48:28.169
Platform VIPs, short JVM DNS TTL, outlier detection.

784
00:48:29.519 --> 00:48:30.607
Observability.

785
00:48:30.887 --> 00:48:41.511
Per dependency, per caller: request rate, errors by type (timeout, 5xx, connection), latency histogram, in-flight concurrency, pool waits, fallbacks served.

786
00:48:41.791 --> 00:48:46.185
Name metrics by dependency so the dashboard answers who is slowing me down?.

787
00:48:46.465 --> 00:48:50.412
Deadline accounting: log or trace the remaining budget at each hop.

788
00:48:50.692 --> 00:48:55.641
A trace showing remaining=12ms at pricing tells you where the time went.

789
00:48:55.921 --> 00:49:01.148
Retries: attempts per request, retry-budget usage, and retries by status code.

790
00:49:02.498 --> 00:49:10.861
Mesh: golden signals per service pair, mTLS coverage (% of traffic encrypted), ejections by outlier detection.

791
00:49:11.141 --> 00:49:17.157
Contracts: counts of unknown fields seen (protobuf) and deserialization errors (JSON).

792
00:49:17.437 --> 00:49:19.591
Both are early warnings of version skew.

793
00:49:20.941 --> 00:49:35.320
Security. mTLS between services with workload identities (mesh or library), plus authorization per caller and endpoint: pricing accepts calls from checkout and the BFFs, not from notification-service.

794
00:49:35.600 --> 00:49:47.184
Propagate the end-user identity as a token (a JWT, or an exchanged token with narrowed scopes), never as a trusted X-User-Id header that any caller can set.

795
00:49:47.464 --> 00:49:54.632
Deadline and retry headers are inputs too: cap them (a client can't ask for a 10-minute budget) and validate them.

796
00:49:55.982 --> 00:50:03.833
Webhooks from external parties: verify signatures, check timestamps against replay, and use allowlists (Ch 23).

797
00:50:05.183 --> 00:50:06.847
Performance and tuning knobs.

798
00:50:07.127 --> 00:50:08.492
Here is a quick table:

799
00:50:08.772 --> 00:50:10.756
Knob, and Default here, and Effect.

800
00:50:11.036 --> 00:50:12.103
Connect timeout.

801
00:50:12.383 --> 00:50:13.300
Default here:

802
00:50:13.580 --> 00:50:14.903
300 ms.

803
00:50:15.183 --> 00:50:15.972
Effect:

804
00:50:16.252 --> 00:50:17.895
Fail fast on dead hosts.

805
00:50:18.175 --> 00:50:19.327
Per-call timeout.

806
00:50:19.607 --> 00:50:23.617
Default here: min(remaining minus margin, dependency cap).

807
00:50:23.897 --> 00:50:24.687
Effect:

808
00:50:24.967 --> 00:50:26.033
Bounded waiting.

809
00:50:26.313 --> 00:50:27.935
Deadline margin per hop.

810
00:50:28.215 --> 00:50:29.132
Default here:

811
00:50:29.412 --> 00:50:30.735
10 to 20 ms.

812
00:50:31.015 --> 00:50:31.804
Effect:

813
00:50:32.084 --> 00:50:33.663
Time to answer your own caller.

814
00:50:33.943 --> 00:50:35.671
Pool size per dependency.

815
00:50:35.951 --> 00:50:36.868
Default here:

816
00:50:37.148 --> 00:50:39.047
Little's law times 1.5.

817
00:50:39.327 --> 00:50:40.116
Effect:

818
00:50:40.396 --> 00:50:42.231
Enough concurrency without flooding.

819
00:50:42.511 --> 00:50:44.217
Concurrency limit (bulkhead).

820
00:50:44.497 --> 00:50:45.415
Default here:

821
00:50:45.695 --> 00:50:47.721
2 to 3 times normal in-flight.

822
00:50:48.001 --> 00:50:48.791
Effect:

823
00:50:49.071 --> 00:50:50.841
Protects the caller and the callee.

824
00:50:51.121 --> 00:50:52.892
Retry attempts or budget.

825
00:50:53.172 --> 00:50:54.089
Default here:

826
00:50:54.369 --> 00:50:57.249
1 retry at the gateway / at most 10 %.

827
00:50:57.529 --> 00:50:58.319
Effect:

828
00:50:58.599 --> 00:51:00.711
Transient fault tolerance without storms.

829
00:51:02.061 --> 00:51:03.021
Protocol.

830
00:51:03.301 --> 00:51:06.522
Default here: gRPC for hot internal paths.

831
00:51:06.802 --> 00:51:07.591
Effect:

832
00:51:07.871 --> 00:51:09.514
Payload size (L3:

833
00:51:09.794 --> 00:51:12.098
2.6x smaller), deadlines.

834
00:51:12.378 --> 00:51:14.661
HTTP or 2 multiplexing.

835
00:51:14.941 --> 00:51:15.858
Default here:

836
00:51:16.138 --> 00:51:17.567
On for gRPC.

837
00:51:17.847 --> 00:51:18.637
Effect:

838
00:51:18.917 --> 00:51:20.090
Fewer connections.

839
00:51:21.440 --> 00:51:22.165
Cost.

840
00:51:22.445 --> 00:51:24.301
Serialization is CPU.

841
00:51:24.581 --> 00:51:32.923
At tens of thousands of internal calls per second, JSON, then protobuf is a measurable CPU and bandwidth saving (L3:

842
00:51:33.203 --> 00:51:36.829
706, then 271 bytes per order).

843
00:51:37.109 --> 00:51:39.456
Cross-AZ calls cost money per GB.

844
00:51:39.736 --> 00:51:44.963
Prefer zone-local routing for chatty paths (locality-aware load balancing in the mesh).

845
00:51:45.243 --> 00:51:51.109
Sidecar meshes add CPU or memory per pod, which is significant at hundreds of pods.

846
00:51:51.389 --> 00:51:53.971
Ambient reduces it for L4-only needs.

847
00:51:54.251 --> 00:51:56.853
Orphan work and retry storms are pure waste.

848
00:51:57.133 --> 00:52:02.424
Deadlines and single-layer retries directly reduce the capacity needed for peak events.

849
00:52:03.774 --> 00:52:04.713
Part 7.

850
00:52:04.993 --> 00:52:06.699
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

851
00:52:07.949 --> 00:52:08.589
1.

852
00:52:08.869 --> 00:52:10.939
No timeout (library defaults).

853
00:52:12.289 --> 00:52:27.478
Here is the Java code. before: waits as long as pricing takes; holds a thread and a connection the whole time (L2 A). after: per-dependency client with connect + call timeouts derived from the remaining deadline, and a fallback.

854
00:52:30.328 --> 00:52:31.032
2.

855
00:52:31.312 --> 00:52:33.445
Timeouts longer than the caller's deadline.

856
00:52:33.725 --> 00:52:34.536
Symptom:

857
00:52:34.816 --> 00:52:38.123
The callee keeps working after the user is gone (L2 B:

858
00:52:38.403 --> 00:52:40.323
89 s of orphan work).

859
00:52:40.603 --> 00:52:41.328
Fix:

860
00:52:41.608 --> 00:52:46.728
Propagate deadlines, and set timeout = min(remaining minus margin, cap).

861
00:52:47.008 --> 00:52:47.755
3.

862
00:52:48.035 --> 00:52:49.507
Retries at every layer.

863
00:52:49.787 --> 00:52:50.597
Symptom:

864
00:52:50.877 --> 00:52:54.547
27x load on the failing dependency (L2 C).

865
00:52:54.827 --> 00:52:55.552
Fix:

866
00:52:55.832 --> 00:53:00.056
One retry layer, retry budgets, and only idempotent operations.

867
00:53:00.336 --> 00:53:01.040
4.

868
00:53:01.320 --> 00:53:04.456
Shared thread or connection pools across dependencies.

869
00:53:04.736 --> 00:53:05.547
Symptom:

870
00:53:05.827 --> 00:53:09.048
One slow dependency blocks unrelated features.

871
00:53:09.328 --> 00:53:10.053
Fix:

872
00:53:10.333 --> 00:53:13.597
Bulkheads: a pool and concurrency limit per dependency.

873
00:53:14.947 --> 00:53:15.715
5.

874
00:53:15.995 --> 00:53:18.726
Synchronous call for something that's really an event.

875
00:53:19.006 --> 00:53:24.169
Symptom: order-service calls notification, invoice and loyalty in the checkout path.

876
00:53:24.449 --> 00:53:25.174
Fix:

877
00:53:25.454 --> 00:53:29.699
Publish order.order.placed.v1 and let them react (Ch 14).

878
00:53:29.979 --> 00:53:30.726
6.

879
00:53:31.006 --> 00:53:34.419
Changing a protobuf field's type or reusing its number.

880
00:53:35.769 --> 00:53:46.713
Here is the PROTOBUF code. before: same number, new type, then readers silently see no value (L3). after: keep the old field, reserve it when retired, add a new number.

881
00:53:49.563 --> 00:53:50.310
7.

882
00:53:50.590 --> 00:53:52.745
Trusting identity headers from callers.

883
00:53:53.025 --> 00:53:53.835
Symptom:

884
00:53:54.115 --> 00:53:57.635
Any service can impersonate any user with X-User-Id.

885
00:53:57.915 --> 00:53:58.641
Fix:

886
00:53:58.921 --> 00:54:01.651
Signed tokens and mTLS identity.

887
00:54:01.931 --> 00:54:02.614
8.

888
00:54:02.894 --> 00:54:06.969
A mesh and the application both retrying and timing out differently.

889
00:54:07.249 --> 00:54:08.059
Symptom:

890
00:54:08.339 --> 00:54:12.307
Confusing behaviour, double retries, timeouts that never fire.

891
00:54:12.587 --> 00:54:13.313
Fix:

892
00:54:13.593 --> 00:54:19.779
Document which layer owns what (§2.9), and set the mesh timeout as an upper ceiling only.

893
00:54:20.059 --> 00:54:20.806
9.

894
00:54:21.086 --> 00:54:24.115
Assuming virtual threads remove the need for limits.

895
00:54:24.395 --> 00:54:25.206
Symptom:

896
00:54:25.486 --> 00:54:28.899
Thousands of concurrent calls flood a dependency (L1).

897
00:54:29.179 --> 00:54:29.905
Fix:

898
00:54:30.185 --> 00:54:32.553
Semaphores and bulkheads per dependency.

899
00:54:33.903 --> 00:54:34.607
10.

900
00:54:34.887 --> 00:54:36.636
Chatty fine-grained APIs.

901
00:54:36.916 --> 00:54:37.727
Symptom:

902
00:54:38.007 --> 00:54:39.820
40 calls to render a cart.

903
00:54:40.100 --> 00:54:40.825
Fix:

904
00:54:41.105 --> 00:54:47.015
Coarse-grained endpoints (batch GetPrices(skus)), composition at a BFF (Ch 16).

905
00:54:48.365 --> 00:54:49.239
Part 8.

906
00:54:49.519 --> 00:54:50.863
Interview Q and A.

907
00:54:52.113 --> 00:54:52.945
Beginner.

908
00:54:53.225 --> 00:54:54.249
Question 1.

909
00:54:54.529 --> 00:54:58.519
When do you call another service synchronously versus asynchronously?

910
00:54:58.799 --> 00:55:00.164
Thirty-second answer:

911
00:55:00.444 --> 00:55:06.759
Synchronously when the caller needs the answer to continue right now (a price, a stock check, a payment result).

912
00:55:07.039 --> 00:55:14.121
Asynchronously when the work can happen later or is a fact others react to (order placed, then invoice, notification).

913
00:55:14.401 --> 00:55:20.503
Synchronous calls couple availability and latency, and asynchronous calls add eventual consistency.

914
00:55:21.853 --> 00:55:22.855
Question 2.

915
00:55:23.135 --> 00:55:25.482
Why must every remote call have a timeout?

916
00:55:25.762 --> 00:55:27.127
Thirty-second answer:

917
00:55:27.407 --> 00:55:32.719
Without one, a slow dependency holds the caller's threads and connections indefinitely.

918
00:55:32.999 --> 00:55:40.210
The caller then runs out of capacity and fails even for requests that don't need that dependency, which is a cascade.

919
00:55:41.560 --> 00:55:42.605
Question 3.

920
00:55:42.885 --> 00:55:44.443
REST or gRPC?

921
00:55:44.723 --> 00:55:46.088
Thirty-second answer:

922
00:55:46.368 --> 00:56:03.989
REST or JSON for external and browser-facing APIs, and wherever simplicity, caching and tooling matter. gRPC or protobuf for internal high-volume calls, polyglot services and streaming, where typed contracts, compact payloads and built-in deadlines help.

923
00:56:05.339 --> 00:56:06.406
Question 4.

924
00:56:06.686 --> 00:56:08.030
What is a service mesh?

925
00:56:08.310 --> 00:56:09.675
Thirty-second answer:

926
00:56:09.955 --> 00:56:26.787
An infrastructure layer that handles service-to-service communication (mTLS, load balancing, retries or timeouts, traffic shifting, telemetry) via proxies (sidecars or node-level or ambient), configured centrally instead of in each service's code.

927
00:56:28.137 --> 00:56:29.140
Intermediate.

928
00:56:29.420 --> 00:56:30.529
Question 5.

929
00:56:30.809 --> 00:56:32.687
How do you choose timeout values?

930
00:56:32.967 --> 00:56:34.332
Thirty-second answer:

931
00:56:34.612 --> 00:56:38.345
Start from the user-facing budget and split it across the call graph.

932
00:56:38.625 --> 00:56:42.103
Parallel calls share a slice, and sequential calls add up.

933
00:56:42.383 --> 00:56:52.772
Each call's timeout is min(remaining deadline minus margin, dependency cap), where the cap is about the dependency's p99.9 plus headroom.

934
00:56:53.052 --> 00:56:55.271
Review with real latency histograms.

935
00:56:56.621 --> 00:56:57.730
Question 6.

936
00:56:58.010 --> 00:56:59.738
What is deadline propagation?

937
00:57:00.018 --> 00:57:01.383
Thirty-second answer:

938
00:57:01.663 --> 00:57:15.871
Passing the remaining time budget along with each request, so every hop limits its own work and downstream timeouts to what's left, and refuses to start work that can't finish. gRPC carries it in grpc-timeout.

939
00:57:16.151 --> 00:57:19.437
For HTTP, use a remaining-milliseconds header.

940
00:57:20.787 --> 00:57:21.875
Question 7.

941
00:57:22.155 --> 00:57:24.117
Which protobuf changes are safe?

942
00:57:24.397 --> 00:57:25.763
Thirty-second answer:

943
00:57:26.043 --> 00:57:33.979
Adding fields with new numbers, renaming fields (names aren't on the wire), and removing fields if their numbers and names are reserved.

944
00:57:34.259 --> 00:57:39.357
Unsafe: changing a field's type or number, reusing numbers, and changing semantics.

945
00:57:40.707 --> 00:57:41.731
Question 8.

946
00:57:42.011 --> 00:57:44.550
How should Spring services call other services?

947
00:57:44.830 --> 00:57:46.195
Thirty-second answer:

948
00:57:46.475 --> 00:58:01.771
Use RestClient (or HTTP interfaces on top of it) for synchronous REST, with a client per dependency that has its own connection pool, connect and response timeouts, a deadline-propagating interceptor, a concurrency limit, and metrics.

949
00:58:02.051 --> 00:58:07.193
Use generated gRPC stubs with withDeadlineAfter for gRPC.

950
00:58:07.473 --> 00:58:09.649
Avoid raw clients with default settings.

951
00:58:10.999 --> 00:58:12.065
Question 9.

952
00:58:12.345 --> 00:58:14.393
Why can retries make an outage worse?

953
00:58:14.673 --> 00:58:16.039
Thirty-second answer:

954
00:58:16.319 --> 00:58:27.156
Retries add load exactly when the dependency is struggling, and when several layers each retry, the load multiplies (3 attempts times 3 layers = 27x).

955
00:58:27.436 --> 00:58:31.255
The dependency can't recover, and latency grows for everyone.

956
00:58:32.605 --> 00:58:33.373
Senior.

957
00:58:33.653 --> 00:58:34.698
Question 10.

958
00:58:34.978 --> 00:58:38.647
Design the calling conventions for a 26-service platform.

959
00:58:38.927 --> 00:58:40.293
Thirty-second answer:

960
00:58:40.573 --> 00:58:42.343
Rules for sync versus async.

961
00:58:42.623 --> 00:58:46.229
REST at the edge, gRPC internally for hot paths.

962
00:58:46.509 --> 00:58:54.573
A platform client library that propagates deadlines, applies per-dependency timeouts, bulkheads and metrics, and declares fallbacks.

963
00:58:54.853 --> 00:58:56.879
Retries at one layer with budgets.

964
00:58:57.159 --> 00:59:00.615
Contract checks in CI. mTLS via the mesh.

965
00:59:01.965 --> 00:59:03.096
Question 11.

966
00:59:03.376 --> 00:59:07.728
How do you prevent a slow optional dependency from hurting critical flows?

967
00:59:08.008 --> 00:59:09.373
Thirty-second answer:

968
00:59:09.653 --> 00:59:22.261
Give it a short timeout within a budget, a fallback (cached or default value plus a flag to the UI), its own pool and concurrency limit (bulkhead), and a circuit breaker to stop calling it while it's failing.

969
00:59:22.541 --> 00:59:25.315
Ideally, make it asynchronous or precomputed.

970
00:59:26.665 --> 00:59:27.753
Question 12.

971
00:59:28.033 --> 00:59:32.299
How do you evolve an internal API without coordinated deploys?

972
00:59:32.579 --> 00:59:33.945
Thirty-second answer:

973
00:59:34.225 --> 00:59:47.985
Make only backward- and forward-compatible changes (additive fields, tolerant readers, no type or number changes), deploy the server first when adding capabilities, and keep old fields until all clients have migrated.

974
00:59:48.265 --> 00:59:51.507
Breaking changes get a new version served in parallel.

975
00:59:51.787 --> 00:59:55.542
Enforce with CI checks and consumer-driven contract tests.

976
00:59:56.892 --> 00:59:58.172
Question 13.

977
00:59:58.452 --> 01:00:01.567
What does a service mesh give you, and what should stay in the app?

978
01:00:01.847 --> 01:00:03.212
Thirty-second answer:

979
01:00:03.492 --> 01:00:16.313
The mesh gives mTLS and workload identity, uniform telemetry, load balancing with outlier detection, and traffic shifting (canary or mirroring), plus optionally retries and timeouts.

980
01:00:16.593 --> 01:00:23.185
The app keeps deadlines (it knows the business budget), fallbacks, idempotency and business-level retries.

981
01:00:23.465 --> 01:00:25.001
Pick one owner per concern.

982
01:00:26.351 --> 01:00:27.546
Question 14.

983
01:00:27.826 --> 01:00:31.410
Virtual threads: what do they change for service-to-service calls?

984
01:00:31.690 --> 01:00:33.055
Thirty-second answer:

985
01:00:33.335 --> 01:00:34.999
Blocking calls become cheap.

986
01:00:35.279 --> 01:00:41.871
Waiting doesn't consume a platform thread, so simple synchronous code scales to many concurrent calls (L1:

987
01:00:42.151 --> 01:00:49.383
4,000 waiting calls in 0.55 s versus 10.1 s on 200 platform threads).

988
01:00:49.663 --> 01:00:55.701
They don't make dependencies faster or bigger, so you still need timeouts, concurrency limits and deadlines.

989
01:00:57.051 --> 01:00:57.947
Architect.

990
01:00:58.227 --> 01:00:59.421
Question 15.

991
01:00:59.701 --> 01:01:03.413
A dependency's latency spiked and your whole platform went down.

992
01:01:03.693 --> 01:01:05.059
Postmortem and fixes?

993
01:01:05.339 --> 01:01:06.704
Thirty-second answer:

994
01:01:06.984 --> 01:01:20.851
The chain: no timeouts, then pool exhaustion in callers, then unrelated endpoints failing, then retries at every layer amplifying load on the slow dependency, then orphan work continuing after users left.

995
01:01:21.131 --> 01:01:32.672
Fixes: timeouts from budgets, bulkheads, deadline propagation, single-layer retries with budgets, circuit breakers, fallbacks for optional features, and dashboards per dependency.

996
01:01:34.022 --> 01:01:35.217
Question 16.

997
01:01:35.497 --> 01:01:38.142
Should the grocery platform adopt a service mesh?

998
01:01:38.422 --> 01:01:39.787
Thirty-second answer:

999
01:01:40.067 --> 01:01:48.025
Yes, for mTLS and telemetry across 26 services owned by 8 teams, in ambient or lightweight mode first.

1000
01:01:48.305 --> 01:01:56.027
Keep retries and deadlines in the app or gateway, and add L7 features (canaries, mirroring) per namespace when needed.

1001
01:01:56.307 --> 01:01:59.657
Measure latency and resource overhead before and after.

1002
01:02:01.007 --> 01:02:02.351
Question 17.

1003
01:02:02.631 --> 01:02:06.343
How do you set end-to-end latency SLOs across service calls?

1004
01:02:06.623 --> 01:02:07.988
Thirty-second answer:

1005
01:02:08.268 --> 01:02:27.169
Define the SLO at the user journey (checkout p99 at most 1.5 s), decompose it into a budget per hop (parallel slices, sequential sums, margins), set dependency SLOs and timeouts from those budgets, and monitor with traces that show where each request's time went.

1006
01:02:27.449 --> 01:02:29.732
Revisit the budgets when the graph changes.

1007
01:02:31.082 --> 01:02:32.255
Question 18.

1008
01:02:32.535 --> 01:02:36.375
When would you use async request-reply instead of a synchronous call?

1009
01:02:36.655 --> 01:02:38.021
Thirty-second answer:

1010
01:02:38.301 --> 01:02:53.703
When the operation takes long or has unpredictable latency (PDF invoices, bulk price updates, KYC checks), when the callee has limited capacity and should pull work at its own pace, or when the caller shouldn't hold resources while waiting.

1011
01:02:53.983 --> 01:03:02.943
Respond with 202 Accepted + a status resource (or a correlation id and a reply topic), and notify on completion.

1012
01:03:04.293 --> 01:03:05.531
Question 19.

1013
01:03:05.811 --> 01:03:10.440
How do you handle calls to external providers (Razorpay, SMS vendors)?

1014
01:03:10.720 --> 01:03:12.085
Thirty-second answer:

1015
01:03:12.365 --> 01:03:17.741
Isolate them behind an adapter service with its own timeouts, bulkheads and circuit breakers.

1016
01:03:18.021 --> 01:03:23.077
Use idempotency keys and treat timeouts as unknown outcomes (Ch 13).

1017
01:03:23.357 --> 01:03:34.749
Run async queues for non-interactive sends, reconcile with the provider's reports, verify webhook signatures, and keep multiple vendors with failover where possible (SMS).

1018
01:03:36.099 --> 01:03:37.209
Question 20.

1019
01:03:37.489 --> 01:03:40.902
How do you make good calling behaviour the default for every team?

1020
01:03:41.182 --> 01:03:42.547
Thirty-second answer:

1021
01:03:42.827 --> 01:04:04.929
Build it into the golden path: a platform client library (timeouts, deadlines, bulkheads, metrics, fallback API), service templates that wire it in, lint and fitness checks that forbid raw clients and missing timeouts, contract checks in CI, mesh policies for mTLS and outlier detection, and dashboards per dependency that come for free.

1022
01:04:06.279 --> 01:04:07.175
Part 9.

1023
01:04:07.455 --> 01:04:08.863
Summary and Practice.

1024
01:04:10.113 --> 01:04:11.201
Key takeaways.

1025
01:04:11.481 --> 01:04:15.662
Sync when an answer is needed now, async for facts and deferrable work.

1026
01:04:15.942 --> 01:04:19.483
Every sync dependency costs availability and capacity.

1027
01:04:19.763 --> 01:04:23.369
Little's law: in-flight requests = rate times latency.

1028
01:04:23.649 --> 01:04:26.934
Slow dependencies consume caller resources (L1:

1029
01:04:27.214 --> 01:04:30.627
10.1 s versus 0.55 s).

1030
01:04:30.907 --> 01:04:36.219
Every call gets a timeout from the budget, and optional data gets a fallback (L2 A:

1031
01:04:36.499 --> 01:04:39.955
20 or 500, then 500 or 500).

1032
01:04:40.235 --> 01:04:44.289
Propagate deadlines, and don't start work that can't finish (L2 B:

1033
01:04:44.569 --> 01:04:47.150
89 s of orphan work, then 0).

1034
01:04:48.500 --> 01:04:53.641
Retry at one layer, with budgets, for idempotent operations only (L2 C:

1035
01:04:53.921 --> 01:04:55.948
27x, then 3x).

1036
01:04:56.228 --> 01:05:01.348
Bulkheads: a pool and a concurrency limit per dependency, virtual threads or not.

1037
01:05:01.628 --> 01:05:05.809
REST at the edge, gRPC for hot internal paths (L3:

1038
01:05:06.089 --> 01:05:10.612
706, then 271 bytes, built-in deadlines).

1039
01:05:10.892 --> 01:05:16.844
Schema evolution: add and rename are safe; type or number changes break silently (L3).

1040
01:05:17.124 --> 01:05:18.233
Check in CI.

1041
01:05:19.583 --> 01:05:23.466
A mesh owns mTLS, telemetry and outlier detection.

1042
01:05:23.746 --> 01:05:26.903
The app owns deadlines, fallbacks and idempotency.

1043
01:05:27.183 --> 01:05:28.421
One retry owner.

1044
01:05:28.701 --> 01:05:32.477
Make it the default via a platform client library and a template.

